<p class="eyebrow">Current Dissertation</p>
<h2>Rising Powers, Unequal Lives</h2>
<p>The Political Economy of Household Gains in China and Vietnam, 2010–2025</p>
<p class="label">Research in progress</p>
<p>Period: 2010–2025<br>Cases: China and Vietnam<br>Outcome: The distribution of real household income gains</p>
</div>
<h2 id="research-question">Research Question</h2>
<p>Why have market earnings and fiscal redistribution contributed differently to household income gains in China and Vietnam, 2010–2025?</p>
<h2>The Comparative Problem</h2>
<p>China and Vietnam have both pursued market-oriented development under one-party rule. I am interested in how the gains from that development reach households through employment, wages and redistribution.</p>
<p>The first task is to establish whether earnings and redistribution have contributed differently in the two countries. I will remain open to similarities, convergence over time or differences that depend on the period and measure used.</p>
<p>The study focuses on real household income gains across income groups within each country. Economic security and national power provide background to the question; they are not separate outcomes being measured.</p>
<h2>A Two-Stage Comparison</h2>
<h3>Comparing Household Gains</h3>
<p>I will use published household statistics and research to compare the roles of earnings, transfers and public services. The analysis will distinguish changes in market income from redistribution, and household cash income from the estimated value of public services.</p>
<h3>Evaluating Political Explanations</h3>
<p>I will then examine whether political incentives, fiscal institutions and policy delivery help explain the income patterns. Other possible explanations include changes in employment, wages and population composition, as well as economic shocks and policy catch-up.</p>
<h2 id="political-mechanisms">Political Mechanisms</h2>
<h3>Officials’ Evaluation and Advancement</h3>
<p>I will compare the incentives shaping officials’ decisions: anti-poverty targets in Chinese cadre evaluation, and investment competition and provincial leadership appointments in Vietnam. In both countries, the analysis will consider central intervention alongside local discretion.</p>
<p>To support an incentives explanation, I will need evidence connecting evaluation criteria to officials’ decisions, resource allocation and household outcomes. An official target alone does not establish that a policy was implemented or had an effect.</p>
<h3>Local Financing and Implementation</h3>
<p>The second mechanism concerns how local responsibilities are financed. In China, I will examine local spending responsibilities and transfers from central government. In Vietnam, I will examine provincial revenue-retention rates, negotiated revenue sharing and balancing transfers.</p>
<p>I will examine how financing rules and budget allocations affect local delivery. Urban–rural registration will form part of the institutional context, without turning the project into a separate study of migration.</p>
<h2 id="methods">Methods and Evidence</h2>
<p>The study will use a structured, focused qualitative comparison of published household statistics, policy decisions and fiscal arrangements. The documents will include selected development plans for 2011–2015, 2016–2020 and 2021–2025, alongside fiscal rules, directives and budgets.</p>
<p>I will trace decisions, resource commitments and revisions in the policy documents, assessing academic research and fiscal-incidence studies separately. An evidence matrix will record observation years, income definitions, proposed mechanisms, alternative explanations and uncertainties.</p>
<p>Fiscal-incidence estimates for 2018 will provide a benchmark within the study period. Earlier and later survey-based findings will help establish income trends within each country. Where the evidence permits, material for 2024–2025 will describe recent household trends and policy developments; it cannot provide a comparable account of redistribution across the full period.</p>
<p>The project draws on published evidence rather than original survey modelling, interviews or participant data.</p>
<h2>Comparability and Limits</h2>
<p>I will compare figures between countries only where their definitions allow it. Income, expenditure and the estimated value of public services capture different aspects of household resources. Survey coverage, price adjustments, pension treatment and observation years also need to be checked.</p>
<p>The comparison can assess whether political explanations fit the observed income patterns, but it cannot isolate their causal effects over the full period. I will seek contradictory findings and check whether apparently separate studies rely on the same survey.</p>
<h2>Contribution and Policy Relevance</h2>
<p>I aim to clarify the relative roles of earnings and redistribution before assessing the political explanations for those patterns. The comparison may identify differences between the countries, convergence over time or a more limited distinction than the original question suggests.</p>
<p>For public policy, the question is when institutions help households benefit from growth and when they compensate for unequal market outcomes. The conclusions will distinguish what the evidence supports from what remains uncertain.</p>
